std.regex.vm — Pike VM executor
================================

The Pike VM simulates a nondeterministic finite automaton
(NFA) by maintaining a *set* of active threads, one per
possible path through the regex. At each input character,
every active thread advances one step. Threads that fail are
discarded; threads that reach `Match` record a successful
match.

This is Ken Thompson's 1968 algorithm, extended by Rob Pike
to track capture group positions. It guarantees $O(n \cdot m)$
time: for each of the $n$ input characters, we process at
most $m$ threads (one per VM instruction), and each
instruction is visited at most once per input position.


POSIX leftmost-longest semantics
---------------------------------

POSIX requires the **leftmost** match, and among leftmost
matches, the **longest**. The Pike VM achieves this by:

1. Starting a new thread at every input position (leftmost).
2. Running all threads in parallel, not stopping at the
   first match (longest).
3. When multiple threads match, keeping the one with the
   best (earliest start, longest span) captures.


Thread representation
---------------------

Each thread is a program counter (index into the
instruction array) plus a set of saved capture positions.
Threads share structure where possible — the `saves` array
is copied only when a `Save` instruction is executed.

    module std.regex

    /* All types and constants are in the same module (std.regex) */

    struct Thread
        pc: int
        saves: []int


VM state
--------

The VM alternates between two thread lists: `current`
(threads to process at this input position) and `next`
(threads that survived and advanced to the next position).
After processing each character, the lists swap.

    struct VM
        insts: []Inst
        classes: []CharClass
        input: string
        n_slots: int

A `visited` bitmap prevents adding duplicate threads at the
same program counter within a single step. Without this, a
`Split` loop could generate exponentially many threads.

    private VM.add_thread(list: []Thread, t: Thread, visited: []bool, sp: int, input_len: int) -> []Thread
        if t.pc < 0 || t.pc >= len(self.insts) then return list
        if visited[t.pc] then return list

        visited[t.pc] = true

        val inst = self.insts[t.pc]

        /* Epsilon transitions: process immediately without consuming input */
        inst.op match
            3 -> /* OP_JUMP */
                return self.add_thread(list, Thread(inst.arg1, t.saves), visited, sp, input_len)
            4 -> /* OP_SPLIT */
                /* Add preferred path first (greedy) */
                var l = self.add_thread(list, Thread(inst.arg1, t.saves), visited, sp, input_len)
                /* Then alternate path */
                return self.add_thread(l, Thread(inst.arg2, t.saves), visited, sp, input_len)
            5 -> /* OP_SAVE */
                val _ns = new [self.n_slots]int
                var new_saves = _ns[:self.n_slots]

                for var i = 0; i < self.n_slots; i++ do new_saves[i] = t.saves[i]

                new_saves[inst.arg1] = -2
                return self.add_thread(list, Thread(t.pc + 1, new_saves), visited, sp, input_len)
            6 -> /* OP_ANCHOR_START — zero-width assertion */
                if sp == 0
                    return self.add_thread(list, Thread(t.pc + 1, t.saves), visited, sp, input_len)
                return list
            7 -> /* OP_ANCHOR_END — zero-width assertion */
                if sp == input_len
                    return self.add_thread(list, Thread(t.pc + 1, t.saves), visited, sp, input_len)
                return list
            _ ->
                /* Non-epsilon: add to list for character processing */
                return append(list, t)

> **Note:** The `Save` instruction above uses `-2` as a
> placeholder. During character processing in `exec`, the
> `-2` sentinels are replaced with the actual input position
> `sp` before the thread is matched against the current
> character.


Character class matching
------------------------

    private VM.class_matches(class_id: int, ch: int) -> bool
        val cls = self.classes[class_id]
        var matched = false

        for var i = 0; i < len(cls.ranges); i++
            if cls.ranges[i].lo <= ch <= cls.ranges[i].hi
                matched = true
                break

        if cls.negated then !matched
        else matched


Execution
---------

The main loop processes one input character per iteration.
At each step:

1. For every thread in `current`, check if the current
   instruction matches the input character.
2. If it matches, advance the thread's PC and add it to
   `next`.
3. If a thread reaches `Match`, record the captures as the
   best match so far.
4. Swap `current` and `next`; clear `next`.

The loop also starts a new thread at PC 0 before each
character, so that matches beginning at any position are
found (unless an earlier match has already been found — for
leftmost semantics).

    exec(insts: []Inst, classes: []CharClass, input: string, n_groups: int) -> Match
        val n_slots = (n_groups + 1) * 2
        val _bs = new [n_groups + 1]int
        var best_starts = _bs[:n_groups + 1]
        val _be = new [n_groups + 1]int
        var best_ends = _be[:n_groups + 1]
        var matched = false

        for var i = 0; i <= n_groups; i++
            best_starts[i] = -1
            best_ends[i] = -1

        var vm: VM

        vm.insts = insts
        vm.classes = classes
        vm.input = input
        vm.n_slots = n_slots

        val _cur = new [16]Thread
        var current = _cur[:0]
        val _nxt = new [16]Thread
        var next_list = _nxt[:0]
        val input_len = len(input)

        /* Process each input position, plus one past the end (for $ anchor and final matches) */
        for var sp = 0; sp <= input_len; sp++
            /* Start a new thread at position sp if we haven't found a match yet */
            if !matched
                val _is = new [n_slots]int
                var init_saves = _is[:n_slots]

                for var j = 0; j < n_slots; j++ do init_saves[j] = -1

                val _v = new [len(insts)]bool
                var visited = _v[:len(insts)]

                current = vm.add_thread(current, Thread(0, init_saves), visited, sp, input_len)

            val ch = if sp < input_len then input[sp] else -1

            /* Process all current threads */
            val _nl = new [16]Thread
            next_list = _nl[:0]

            for var i = 0; i < len(current); i++
                val t = current[i]
                val inst = insts[t.pc]

                /* Fix up Save placeholders: replace -2 with actual position */
                for var s = 0; s < n_slots; s++
                    if t.saves[s] == -2 then t.saves[s] = sp

                inst.op match
                    0 -> /* OP_CHAR */
                        if ch == inst.arg1
                            val _v = new [len(insts)]bool
                            var visited = _v[:len(insts)]

                            next_list = vm.add_thread(next_list, Thread(t.pc + 1, t.saves), visited, sp + 1, input_len)
                    1 -> /* OP_ANY */
                        if ch >= 0
                            val _v = new [len(insts)]bool
                            var visited = _v[:len(insts)]

                            next_list = vm.add_thread(next_list, Thread(t.pc + 1, t.saves), visited, sp + 1, input_len)
                    2 -> /* OP_MATCH */
                        /* Record this match if it's better than the current best */
                        if !matched || t.saves[0] < best_starts[0] || (t.saves[0] == best_starts[0] && sp > best_ends[0])
                            matched = true

                            for var g = 0; g <= n_groups; g++
                                best_starts[g] = t.saves[g * 2]
                                best_ends[g] = t.saves[g * 2 + 1]

                            /* For group 0, end = current position */
                            best_ends[0] = sp
                    8 -> /* OP_CLASS */
                        if ch >= 0 && vm.class_matches(inst.arg1, ch)
                            val _v = new [len(insts)]bool
                            var visited = _v[:len(insts)]

                            next_list = vm.add_thread(next_list, Thread(t.pc + 1, t.saves), visited, sp + 1, input_len)
                    _ -> return

            /* Swap lists */
            current = next_list

            /* If no threads left and we have a match, we're done */
            if len(current) == 0 && matched then break

        Match(best_starts, best_ends, n_groups + 1)


Tests
-----

End-to-end tests: parse a BRE pattern, compile to bytecode,
execute against an input string, and verify match results.

> All end-to-end tests pass: the Pike VM correctly handles
> recursive `add_thread` calls for epsilon transitions
> (jump, split, save, anchors).

    /* parse_bre and compile are in the same module */


### Helper

A convenience function that parses, compiles, and executes
in one step, returning the Match result.

    match_bre(pattern: string, input: string) -> Match
        root, nodes, classes, n_groups, err, _, _ = parse_bre(pattern)

        assert(!err, s"pattern '$pattern' should parse")

        val insts = compile(root, nodes, n_groups)

        exec(insts, classes, input, n_groups)


### Literal match

    #test
    test_match_literal() -> void
        val m = match_bre("hello", "say hello world")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 4, s"start=4, got $start")
        assert(en == 9, s"end=9, got $en")


### Dot matches any character

    #test
    test_match_dot() -> void
        val m = match_bre("h.llo", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")

        start, _ = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 0, "starts at 0")


### Anchored match

    #test
    test_match_anchored() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^hello$", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "anchored match")

    #test
    test_match_anchored_fail() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^hello$", "say hello")

        assert(!m.matched(), "anchored should not match")


### Star — zero or more

    #test
    test_match_star_zero() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^ab*c$", "ac")

        assert(m.matched(), "b* matches zero b's")

    #test
    test_match_star_many() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^ab*c$", "abbbc")

        assert(m.matched(), "b* matches three b's")


### Capture groups

    #test
    test_match_capture() -> void
        val m = match_bre("\\(hello\\)", "say hello world")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")
        assert(m.n_groups == 2, "group 0 + group 1")

        start, en = m.group(1)

        assert(start == 4, s"group 1 start=4, got $start")
        assert(en == 9, s"group 1 end=9, got $en")


### Character class

    #test
    test_match_class() -> void
        val m = match_bre("[aeiou]", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match vowel")

        start, _ = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 1, "first vowel at 1 (e)")


### No match

    #test
    test_no_match() -> void
        val m = match_bre("xyz", "hello")

        assert(!m.matched(), "should not match")


### Leftmost-longest

    #test
    test_leftmost_longest() -> void
        val m = match_bre("a*", "aaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 0, "leftmost")
        assert(en == 3, s"longest: end=3, got $en")


### Complex pattern

    #test
    test_match_complex() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^\\([a-z]*\\)@\\([a-z]*\\)$", "user@host")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 0, "group 1 start")
        assert(e1 == 4, "group 1 end")

        s2, e2 = m.group(2)

        assert(s2 == 5, "group 2 start")
        assert(e2 == 9, "group 2 end")


Extended tests
--------------

Tests for features beyond the basics: alternation, `+`, `?`,
groups, intervals, named classes, escaping, and edge cases.


### Helper

    match_regex(pattern: string, input: string) -> Match
        root, nodes, classes, n_groups, err, _, _ = parse_regex(pattern)

        assert(!err, s"pattern '$pattern' should parse")

        val insts = compile(root, nodes, n_groups)

        exec(insts, classes, input, n_groups)


### Alternation — basic

    #test
    test_ere_alt_first() -> void
        val m = match_regex("cat|dog", "the cat sat")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match cat")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 4, s"start=4, got $start")
        assert(en == 7, s"end=7, got $en")

    #test
    test_ere_alt_second() -> void
        val m = match_regex("cat|dog", "the dog sat")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match dog")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 4, s"start=4, got $start")
        assert(en == 7, s"end=7, got $en")

    #test
    test_ere_alt_no_match() -> void
        val m = match_regex("cat|dog", "the bird sat")

        assert(!m.matched(), "should not match")


### Alternation — leftmost wins

    #test
    test_ere_alt_leftmost() -> void
        val m = match_regex("a|ab", "ab")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 0, "leftmost at 0")


### Multi-way alternation

    #test
    test_ere_alt_three() -> void
        val m = match_regex("red|green|blue", "the blue sky")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match blue")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 4, s"start=4, got $start")
        assert(en == 8, s"end=8, got $en")

    #test
    test_ere_alt_first_of_three() -> void
        val m = match_regex("red|green|blue", "a red car")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match red")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 2, s"start=2, got $start")
        assert(en == 5, s"end=5, got $en")


### Plus quantifier

    #test
    test_ere_plus_one() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^ab+c$", "abc")

        assert(m.matched(), "b+ matches one b")

    #test
    test_ere_plus_many() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^ab+c$", "abbbbc")

        assert(m.matched(), "b+ matches four b's")

    #test
    test_ere_plus_zero_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^ab+c$", "ac")

        assert(!m.matched(), "b+ requires at least one b")


### Question quantifier

    #test
    test_ere_quest_zero() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^ab?c$", "ac")

        assert(m.matched(), "b? matches zero b's")

    #test
    test_ere_quest_one() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^ab?c$", "abc")

        assert(m.matched(), "b? matches one b")

    #test
    test_ere_quest_two_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^ab?c$", "abbc")

        assert(!m.matched(), "b? doesn't match two b's")


### Unescaped groups

    #test
    test_ere_group_capture() -> void
        val m = match_regex("(hello) (world)", "say hello world")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 4, s"group 1 start=4, got $s1")
        assert(e1 == 9, s"group 1 end=9, got $e1")

        s2, e2 = m.group(2)

        assert(s2 == 10, s"group 2 start=10, got $s2")
        assert(e2 == 15, s"group 2 end=15, got $e2")


### Groups with quantifiers

    #test
    test_ere_group_star() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(ab)*$", "ababab")

        assert(m.matched(), "(ab)* matches three repetitions")

    #test
    test_ere_group_star_zero() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(ab)*$", "")

        assert(m.matched(), "(ab)* matches zero repetitions")

    #test
    test_ere_group_plus() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(ab)+$", "ababab")

        assert(m.matched(), "(ab)+ matches three repetitions")

    #test
    test_ere_group_plus_zero_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(ab)+$", "")

        assert(!m.matched(), "(ab)+ requires at least one")

    #test
    test_ere_group_quest() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a(bc)?d$", "ad")

        assert(m.matched(), "(bc)? matches zero")

    #test
    test_ere_group_quest_one() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a(bc)?d$", "abcd")

        assert(m.matched(), "(bc)? matches one")


### Alternation within groups

    #test
    test_ere_group_alt() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(cat|dog)s$", "cats")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match cats")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 0, "group 1 starts at 0")
        assert(e1 == 3, "group 1 ends at 3")

    #test
    test_ere_group_alt_second() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(cat|dog)s$", "dogs")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match dogs")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 0, "group 1 starts at 0")
        assert(e1 == 3, "group 1 ends at 3")


### Anchored ERE

    #test
    test_ere_anchored() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^hello$", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "anchored match")

    #test
    test_ere_anchored_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^hello$", "say hello")

        assert(!m.matched(), "anchored should not match")


### Dot in ERE

    #test
    test_ere_dot() -> void
        val m = match_regex("h.llo", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "dot matches")

    #test
    test_ere_dot_star() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^h.*o$", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), ".* matches ell")


### Character classes in ERE

    #test
    test_ere_class() -> void
        val m = match_regex("[0-9]+", "abc123def")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match digits")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 3, s"start=3, got $start")
        assert(en == 6, s"end=6, got $en")

    #test
    test_ere_negated_class() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[^aeiou]+$", "bcd")

        assert(m.matched(), "consonants only")

    #test
    test_ere_negated_class_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[^aeiou]+$", "bad")

        assert(!m.matched(), "contains vowel")


### Escaped special characters in ERE

    #test
    test_ere_escaped_paren() -> void
        val m = match_regex("\\(a\\)", "(a)")

        assert(m.matched(), "literal parens")

    #test
    test_ere_escaped_pipe() -> void
        val m = match_regex("a\\|b", "a|b")

        assert(m.matched(), "literal pipe")

    #test
    test_ere_escaped_plus() -> void
        val m = match_regex("a\\+", "a+")

        assert(m.matched(), "literal plus")

    #test
    test_ere_escaped_quest() -> void
        val m = match_regex("a\\?", "a?")

        assert(m.matched(), "literal question")

    #test
    test_ere_escaped_dot() -> void
        val m = match_regex("a\\.b", "a.b")

        assert(m.matched(), "literal dot")

    #test
    test_ere_escaped_dot_no_match() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a\\.b$", "axb")

        assert(!m.matched(), "escaped dot doesn't match x")


### Complex ERE patterns

    #test
    test_ere_email() -> void
        val m = match_regex("[a-z]+@[a-z]+\\.[a-z]+", "send to user@host.com please")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match email")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 8, s"start=8, got $start")
        assert(en == 21, s"end=21, got $en")

    #test
    test_ere_ip_like() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+$", "192.168.1.1")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match IP")

    #test
    test_ere_complex_alt_groups() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(https?|ftp)://([a-z]+)$", "http://host")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match URL")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 0, "group 1 start")
        assert(e1 == 4, "group 1 end")

        s2, e2 = m.group(2)

        assert(s2 == 7, s"group 2 start=7, got $s2")
        assert(e2 == 11, s"group 2 end=11, got $e2")

    #test
    test_ere_complex_alt_groups_https() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(https?|ftp)://([a-z]+)$", "https://host")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match https URL")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 0, "group 1 start")
        assert(e1 == 5, s"group 1 end=5, got $e1")

    #test
    test_ere_complex_alt_groups_ftp() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(https?|ftp)://([a-z]+)$", "ftp://files")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match ftp URL")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 0, "group 1 start")
        assert(e1 == 3, s"group 1 end=3, got $e1")


### Leftmost-longest with ERE

    #test
    test_ere_leftmost_longest() -> void
        val m = match_regex("a+", "baaab")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 1, "leftmost at 1")
        assert(en == 4, s"longest: end=4, got $en")


### No match cases

    #test
    test_ere_no_match_alt() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(abc|def)$", "xyz")

        assert(!m.matched(), "neither branch matches")

    #test
    test_ere_no_match_complex() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[A-Z][a-z]+$", "hello")

        assert(!m.matched(), "must start with uppercase")


### Edge cases

    #test
    test_ere_empty_alt_branch() -> void
        /* a| means "a or empty string" — empty always matches */
        val m = match_regex("a|", "b")

        assert(m.matched(), "empty branch matches anything")

    #test
    test_ere_nested_groups() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^((a)(b))$", "ab")

        assert(m.matched(), "should match")
        assert(m.n_groups == 4, s"4 groups (0+3), got ${m.n_groups}")

        s1, e1 = m.group(1)

        assert(s1 == 0, "group 1 start")
        assert(e1 == 2, s"group 1 end=2, got $e1")

        s2, e2 = m.group(2)

        assert(s2 == 0, "group 2 start")
        assert(e2 == 1, s"group 2 end=1, got $e2")

        s3, e3 = m.group(3)

        assert(s3 == 1, "group 3 start")
        assert(e3 == 2, s"group 3 end=2, got $e3")

    #test
    test_ere_star_of_class() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[a-z]*$", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "all lowercase")

    #test
    test_ere_plus_of_class() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[a-z]+$", "")

        assert(!m.matched(), "empty fails plus")

    #test
    test_ere_only_anchor_start() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^abc", "abcdef")

        assert(m.matched(), "prefix match")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 0, "at start")
        assert(en == 3, s"end=3, got $en")

    #test
    test_ere_only_anchor_end() -> void
        val m = match_regex("def$", "abcdef")

        assert(m.matched(), "suffix match")

        start, en = m.group(0)

        assert(start == 3, s"start=3, got $start")
        assert(en == 6, s"end=6, got $en")


### Interval — exact count

    #test
    test_interval_exact() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{3}$", "aaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{3} matches aaa")

    #test
    test_interval_exact_fail_short() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{3}$", "aa")

        assert(!m.matched(), "a{3} doesn't match aa")

    #test
    test_interval_exact_fail_long() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{3}$", "aaaa")

        assert(!m.matched(), "a{3} doesn't match aaaa")


### Interval — range

    #test
    test_interval_range_min() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,4}$", "aa")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{2,4} matches aa")

    #test
    test_interval_range_mid() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,4}$", "aaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{2,4} matches aaa")

    #test
    test_interval_range_max() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,4}$", "aaaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{2,4} matches aaaa")

    #test
    test_interval_range_below() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,4}$", "a")

        assert(!m.matched(), "a{2,4} doesn't match a")

    #test
    test_interval_range_above() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,4}$", "aaaaa")

        assert(!m.matched(), "a{2,4} doesn't match aaaaa")


### Interval — unbounded

    #test
    test_interval_unbounded() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,}$", "aaaaaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{2,} matches 6 a's")

    #test
    test_interval_unbounded_exact() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,}$", "aa")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{2,} matches exactly 2")

    #test
    test_interval_unbounded_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{2,}$", "a")

        assert(!m.matched(), "a{2,} doesn't match 1")


### Interval — zero minimum

    #test
    test_interval_zero_min() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{0,2}$", "")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{0,2} matches empty")

    #test
    test_interval_zero_min_one() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{0,2}$", "a")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{0,2} matches a")

    #test
    test_interval_zero_min_max() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{0,2}$", "aa")

        assert(m.matched(), "a{0,2} matches aa")

    #test
    test_interval_zero_min_above() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^a{0,2}$", "aaa")

        assert(!m.matched(), "a{0,2} doesn't match aaa")


### Interval on group

    #test
    test_interval_group() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(ab){3}$", "ababab")

        assert(m.matched(), "(ab){3} matches ababab")

    #test
    test_interval_group_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^(ab){3}$", "abab")

        assert(!m.matched(), "(ab){3} doesn't match abab")


### Interval in context

    #test
    test_interval_in_pattern() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$", "555-1234")

        assert(m.matched(), "phone number pattern")

    #test
    test_interval_in_pattern_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$", "55-1234")

        assert(!m.matched(), "too few area digits")


### BRE intervals (via match_bre)

    #test
    test_bre_interval_exact() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^a\\{3\\}$", "aaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "BRE a\\{3\\} matches aaa")

    #test
    test_bre_interval_range() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^a\\{2,4\\}$", "aaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "BRE a\\{2,4\\} matches aaa")

    #test
    test_bre_interval_unbounded() -> void
        val m = match_bre("^a\\{2,\\}$", "aaaa")

        assert(m.matched(), "BRE a\\{2,\\} matches aaaa")


### POSIX named character classes

    #test
    test_named_class_alpha() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:alpha:]]+$", "Hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "[:alpha:] matches letters")

    #test
    test_named_class_alpha_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:alpha:]]+$", "Hello123")

        assert(!m.matched(), "[:alpha:] rejects digits")

    #test
    test_named_class_digit() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:digit:]]+$", "12345")

        assert(m.matched(), "[:digit:] matches digits")

    #test
    test_named_class_digit_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:digit:]]+$", "123abc")

        assert(!m.matched(), "[:digit:] rejects letters")

    #test
    test_named_class_alnum() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:alnum:]]+$", "Hello123")

        assert(m.matched(), "[:alnum:] matches letters and digits")

    #test
    test_named_class_alnum_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:alnum:]]+$", "Hello 123")

        assert(!m.matched(), "[:alnum:] rejects space")

    #test
    test_named_class_upper() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:upper:]]+$", "HELLO")

        assert(m.matched(), "[:upper:] matches uppercase")

    #test
    test_named_class_upper_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:upper:]]+$", "Hello")

        assert(!m.matched(), "[:upper:] rejects lowercase")

    #test
    test_named_class_lower() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:lower:]]+$", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "[:lower:] matches lowercase")

    #test
    test_named_class_space() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:space:]]+$", " \t")

        assert(m.matched(), "[:space:] matches whitespace")

    #test
    test_named_class_xdigit() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:xdigit:]]+$", "0123456789abcdefABCDEF")

        assert(m.matched(), "[:xdigit:] matches hex digits")

    #test
    test_named_class_xdigit_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:xdigit:]]+$", "0123g")

        assert(!m.matched(), "[:xdigit:] rejects g")


### Named class combined with ranges

    #test
    test_named_class_combined() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[[:digit:]_]+$", "123_456")

        assert(m.matched(), "digits and underscore")

    #test
    test_named_class_negated() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[^[:digit:]]+$", "hello")

        assert(m.matched(), "not-digits matches letters")

    #test
    test_named_class_negated_fail() -> void
        val m = match_regex("^[^[:digit:]]+$", "hello123")

        assert(!m.matched(), "not-digits rejects digits")
